Upgrading Nagios Core
=====================

Modern web interface security changes
-------------------------------------

The optional "corewindow" argument no longer accepts external URLs. When it
is enabled at configure time, it is restricted to absolute paths inside the
configured Nagios HTML and CGI URL spaces. Existing bookmarks that framed an
unrelated same-origin or external application now open the Nagios overview.

Command forms use 256-bit tokens. HTTPS installations use a host-only Secure
cookie and browser command POSTs with an Origin or Referer header must match
the Nagios origin. Basic authentication should always be protected by HTTPS.
If a trusted reverse proxy terminates TLS, configure it to expose HTTPS=on or
REQUEST_SCHEME=https to the CGI environment without trusting an unsanitized
client X-Forwarded-Proto header.

The modern interface is installed by default. The install-classicui and
install-exfoliation targets remain available for legacy CGI presentation.

nagios-4.3.0 - 2017-02-21
-------------------------

If you use "corewindow", as in
	http://localhost/nagios?corewindow=www.somewhere.com
note that this functionality has been disabled by default in this
release. If you still want to use it, run ./configure with the
parameter --enable-corewindow


nagios-3.0
----------

The HTML documentation covers what you need to know in order
to upgrade from Nagios 2.x  You can find the documentation in
the following subdirectory:

	html/docs

Make sure to read the following sections:

- "What's New" (whatsnew.html)
